Each subject appears once per visit. `day` is the time of that visit (in days from
enrollment), and the other columns hold the lab values measured that day. `futime` and
`status` record the final outcome and are the same for every row belonging to the same
subject.

## Building the counting-process dataset

`split_episodes()` takes the per-subject baseline table and the repeated-measurements
table and merges them into the counting-process long format. The `id` column links the two.
`visit_time` names the column in `visits` that carries measurement times.

::: {#b269bd69 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pandas as pd

# Build a per-subject baseline: one row per subject with follow-up time and outcome.
# pbcseq repeats futime/status for every visit, so drop_duplicates gives one row per id.
pbcseq_pd = gw.load_dataset("pbcseq", backend="pandas")
base = (
    pbcseq_pd.drop_duplicates("id")[["id", "futime", "status"]]
             .rename(columns={"futime": "time"})
)

# Split into counting-process intervals, carrying bilirubin, albumin, and protime forward.
